WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.497
Warning! Warning!

2
00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:06.705
I built this with AI and text to speech, because I can't narrate for shit!

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.165
Hey, Dreamer here.

4
00:00:09.165 --> 00:00:13.127
Today, triggers: how you make things actually happen in your world.

5
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:16.919
Open Mechanics, then Advanced, then Triggers.

6
00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:20.631
A trigger is a rule: when all its conditions pass, its effects happen.

7
00:00:20.631 --> 00:00:22.539
Voyage starts every world with nine,

8
00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.629
all about how characters react when you're kind or friendly to them, and I've added two of ours.

9
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:33.425
Bramble in the roses has recurring switched off, so it fires once and it's done.

10
00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:37.645
Switch it on and it can fire every turn its conditions are met.

11
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.358
Its conditions all have to pass.

12
00:00:40.358 --> 00:00:44.593
The first is mechanical: the party is in the mayor's garden.

13
00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:47.980
Mechanical checks are exact, and they run first.

14
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:54.715
The second is a story check: the AI reads the story and judges whether the player has found Bramble there.

15
00:00:54.715 --> 00:00:57.797
Story checks only run once the mechanical ones pass,

16
00:00:57.797 --> 00:01:03.190
and there's a limited number of them each turn, so always pair one with a mechanical check.

17
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:04.755
Then the effects.

18
00:01:04.755 --> 00:01:11.157
This one's an instruction for the narrator: Bramble's asleep in the mayor's prize roses, snoring.

19
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.595
And this one takes ten off the mayor's relationship score.

20
00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:20.760
No guessing here: when the conditions pass, the effects happen, every time.

21
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:25.686
Back in the triggers list, open running on empty, our second one.

22
00:01:25.686 --> 00:01:29.096
This one's recurring, so instead of firing once and being done,

23
00:01:29.096 --> 00:01:31.803
it can fire every single turn its condition holds.

24
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:35.175
One condition: stamina at zero or below.

25
00:01:35.175 --> 00:01:39.175
A resource check passes if anyone in the party matches.

26
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:45.938
And the effect tells the narrator whoever's out is spent, every turn they stay at zero.

27
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:51.429
So when do you use a trigger instead of just telling the narrator?

28
00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:55.215
Instructions are the narrator's call, and it can miss things.

29
00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:58.132
A trigger fires every time its conditions pass.

30
00:01:58.132 --> 00:02:00.987
If something has to happen, make it a trigger.

31
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.088
That's triggers 101.

32
00:02:04.088 --> 00:02:07.041
Ours are linked in the description.

33
00:02:07.041 --> 00:02:13.116
Next time, triggers 102: phases, flags, and which player a trigger hits.

34
00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:17.515
I'm Dreamer. Now go build something weird, and I'll see you next time!

